        "The Sepsis-3 task force proposed qSOFA as a bedside prompt to identify patients at risk of poor outcome outside the ICU [1]. In a prospective multicentre ED cohort, qSOFA had an area under the ROC curve of 0.80 for in-hospital mortality, compared with 0.65 for SIRS [4]. However, the Surviving Sepsis Campaign 2021 recommends against using qSOFA alone, compared with SIRS, NEWS or MEWS, as a single screening tool for sepsis [2].",
        "In Saudi Arabia, a retrospective study of 444 ED patients enrolled in a sepsis protocol in Riyadh found that a NEWS of 5 or more was highly sensitive (over 88%) but poorly specific for hyperlactataemia, ICU admission or mortality [5]. NEWS2, the updated version, has not been widely evaluated in Saudi EDs.",
