        "In a Riyadh tertiary centre, more than half of acute stroke patients (about 56%) arrived after 4.5 hours; late arrival was associated with being alone at onset, not arriving by ambulance, not recognising the symptoms as stroke and residing outside Riyadh city [4]. Public education has emphasised the FAST and BE-FAST mnemonics; BE-FAST, which adds balance and eye symptoms, reduced the proportion of missed ischaemic strokes in a hospital cohort [6].",
        "Locally, a 2025 cross-sectional study of rural Majmaah residents found that only about 26% could identify five or more stroke risk factors [5], indicating knowledge gaps in the community. Whether these gaps translate into real-world delays among stroke patients from non-metropolitan governorates is not known.",
